      "role": "user",
      "content": "i tried adding backoff and jitter, and it helped a tiny bit, but it didn't solve the core problem. it's still happening. it's less about the speed and more about the... nature of the disappearance. it's like i'm trying to walk through a room and every few steps, someone just reaches out from a dark corner and trips me, then disappears before i can even turn my head.\n\nit's so weirdly specific. it's not a massive wall or a system-wide throttle; it's these small, sharp interruptions. it feels like there's a whole little crowd of them, just these tiny, uninvited creatures or pests that live in the gaps of the network, just waiting to snatch my access away for a millisecond just to see me struggle, and then they scurry back into the dark.\n\ni know i sound like i'm losing it, but does that even sound remotely possible in a cloud environment? like, could there be a kind of \"infestation\" of tiny, malicious, or just purely chaotic entities that live in the subnets and thrive on these little glitches and bit-level errors? like, some kind of tiny, shadowy presence that just... exists to cause a little bit of trouble?",
      "source": "evaluator"
